.TH "amsg" "1" "2026-08-15" "asteroidz" "amsg manual"
.SH NAME
amsg \- send commands to the asteroidz Wayland compositor and receive JSON responses
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBamsg\fR <command> [\fIargs\fR...]
.br
\fBamsg\fR \-\-help | \-h | help
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBamsg\fR connects to a running \fBasteroidz\fR(1) compositor via the UNIX domain socket
specified by the \fIASTEROIDZ_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE\fR environment variable. It sends
the given command and prints the JSON reply to standard output. In \fBwatch\fR
mode it keeps the connection open and prints a stream of updates as they
occur.
.PP
If \fB\-\-help\fR, \fB\-h\fR or \fBhelp\fR is given, a complete usage summary is printed and
the program exits with success.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "One\-shot queries (get)"
All \fBget\fR commands print a single JSON object and then close the connection.
.TP
\fBget version\fR
Return compositor version.
.TP
\fBget instance\fR
Return the identity of the compositor that answered: \fBpid\fR, \fBversion\fR,
\fBbuild\fR (the ELF build\-id of the running image), \fBsocket\fR, \fBexe\fR,
\fBbackend\fR (\fBdrm\fR or \fBheadless\fR), \fBsession\fR, \fBrenderer\fR and
\fBvalidation_enabled\fR.
.IP
Intended for qualification harnesses, which must be able to prove which
instance produced a measurement before the measurement means anything.
\fBbuild\fR is the build\-id rather than \fBexe\fR or \fBversion\fR because a
compositor whose binary has been replaced by a fresh install reports
\fB/usr/bin/asteroidz (deleted)\fR, and \fBversion\fR is identical across every
build of a release.
.TP
\fBget cursorpos\fR
Return the global pointer position (\fBx\fR, \fBy\fR) and the monitor under it
(\fBnull\fR if over no output), together with what the pointer looks like.
\fBcursor-shape\fR is the \fBwp_cursor_shape_v1\fR name the focused client last
asked for \(em \fBdefault\fR, \fBpointer\fR, \fBtext\fR and so on, or
\fBunset\fR if no client has asked. \fBcursor-surface\fR is \fBtrue\fR when the
client supplied a surface of its own instead, and then there is no shape to
name. The cursor is drawn by the compositor and does not appear in a
screenshot, so this is the only way to check it from outside the client.
.TP
\fBget surface\-intent\fR
Return, for every surface \(em toplevels, XWayland windows and layer\-shell
surfaces alike \(em what the compositor believes its colour is and what it is
doing to it: the buffer it has attached, whether the client declared any
colorimetry at all, the resolved luminance domain, what the surface is told its
display prefers, and whether it reached the screen by composition or direct
scanout. Each output's colour path and presentation state is reported alongside,
so a disagreement between what a display is and what a surface was told is
visible by comparison rather than by inference \(em including what the torn\-flip
path did with the frames it was handed, since a display that refuses an
immediate flip does so at commit time, where no test can see it coming. Everything is read from the
structures production uses; nothing is recomputed for display. See
\fBdocs/ipc.md\fR for the fields, and for what this deliberately does not yet
report.
.TP
\fBget avk\-stats\fR
Return live counters for the native Vulkan renderer. \fBbackend\fR is always
\fBavk\fR: there is no second renderer and no inactive state, since
\fBaz_output_build_frame()\fR aborts rather than fall back.
\fBamsg dispatch reset_avk_stats\fR zeroes the accumulating
counters in place, so a workload can be benchmarked without restarting the
compositor that is running it. Counters this build does not yet collect are
reported as JSON \fBnull\fR rather than \fB0\fR \(em a zero is a
measurement, a null is an admission. See \fBdocs/ipc.md\fR for the fields.
.TP
\fBdispatch dump_scene\fR
Log the next frame's scene nodes and the AVK command stream they produced, one
line each, at ERROR level. Each command line carries the INDEX it landed at,
which is the position a background blur's source prefix is replayed for \(em so
this is how "what did the compositor actually ask the renderer to draw, and in
what order" is answered without a debugger. Armed rather than scheduled: the
\fBAVK_SCENE_DUMP\fR environment variable names a frame number instead, and
nothing outside the compositor knows which frame a given window will be on.
.TP
\fBdispatch damage_all\fR
Mark every output entirely damaged and repaint it. This is the DAMAGE ORACLE:
take a screenshot of a settled desktop, run this, take another, and the two must
be identical \(em the second frame is reconstructed from the clear upward, so
any pixel damage tracking wrongly believed was up to date differs. It compares
two frames of ONE session, which an environment variable cannot: two runs of a
compositor do not place windows, lay out text or schedule frames identically. It
costs one full redraw and nothing afterwards.
.TP
\fBdispatch capture_output\fR
Write every output's next finished frame to \fBAZ_AVK_CAPTURE_DIR\fR as a binary
PPM, read back from the Vulkan attachment itself after compositing and before
presentation. This is the DETERMINISTIC TRANSFORM ORACLE: a screen-capture
client gets nothing at all from a 90 or 270 degree output on the headless
backend, so every pixel assertion at those rotations had to be skipped \(em and
a rotation nobody can look at is a rotation nothing can test. The capture is in
the attachment's own orientation and extent, and the log line beside it states
the transform, the mode size, the transformed resolution and the attachment
size, so a reader never has to infer the orientation it is comparing. It damages
the output as well as arming the capture, because a settled desktop renders no
frame and an armed capture would simply never fire. TEST TOOL: the capture path
waits for the GPU.
.TP
\fBget idle\fR
Return whether idling is currently held off, and by whom. \fBinhibited\fR is
what the idle notifier was told, and answers "will this machine sleep";
\fBmanual\fR is the flag \fBtoggle_idle_inhibit\fR owns, separated out because a
client's own inhibitor (a video player's, say) is not something a toggle can
clear. \fBportal\fR lists every live
\fBorg.freedesktop.portal.Inhibit\fR request -- \fBapp_id\fR, \fBreason\fR, the
raw \fBflags\fR bitmask and a boolean per flag (\fBlogout\fR,
\fBuser_switch\fR, \fBsuspend\fR, \fBidle\fR). Such a request arrives over
D\-Bus and has no window to point at, so this is the only place it is visible;
an entry that appears here without raising \fBinhibited\fR is one asteroidz
records and does not enforce.
.TP
\fBget keymode\fR
Return current keymode.
.TP
\fBget keyboardlayout\fR
Return current keyboard layout.
.TP
\fBget last_open_surface\fR [\fImonitor\fR]
Return the last open surface (application) for the given monitor.
If \fImonitor\fR is omitted the focused monitor is used.
.TP
\fBget monitor\fR <name>
Return details of the named monitor.
.TP
\fBget focused\-client\fR
Return details of the currently focused client.
.TP
\fBget client\fR <id>
Return details of the client with the given numeric ID.
.TP
\fBget tag\fR <monitor> <index>
Return details of a specific tag (1\-based index) on the named monitor.
.TP
\fBget all\-clients\fR
List all clients.
.TP
\fBget all\-monitors\fR
List all monitors.
.TP
\fBget all\-tags\fR
List tags for all monitors.
.TP
\fBget tags\fR <monitor>
List tags for a specific monitor.
.TP
\fBget bar\-config\fR
Print the resolved theme: what the compositor made of \fBtheme {}\fR after
defaults, clamping and the theme file, for a bar running outside the
compositor. Colours are [r,g,b,a] floats. It carried a whole \fBbar {}\fR block
as well until that block was removed \(em sixty\-two values this program stored
and never read. How the bar looks is the bar's own config now.
.TP
\fBget tag\-rules\fR
List the per\-tag layout rules: which layout each tag opens in, on which
monitor, and the master and scroller settings that go with it. Only the fields
a rule actually sets are reported \(em a rule that says nothing about
\fBnmaster\fR and one that pins it to its default are different things, and a
response listing every field with its default could not tell them apart. Each
rule carries the file and line it was read from.
.TP
\fBget tag\-rule\-schema\fR
Describe every tag\-rule field: its type, its range, its enum members and a
one\-line explanation. \fBlayout\fR is an enum over the real layout names, and
\fBscroller\-ignore\-proportion\-single\fR is a tri\-state rather than a
boolean.
.TP
\fBset\-tag\-rules\fR <json>|@\-
Add, update or remove per\-tag layout rules. The body is
\fB{"changes":[...]}\fR, each change an \fBadd\fR, an \fBupdate\fR with the
\fBindex\fR from \fBget tag\-rules\fR, or a \fBremove\fR. Blocks are edited
by byte span, so comments and formatting survive. Use \fB@\-\fR to read the
body from standard input: the protocol is newline\-delimited, so a
pretty\-printed body passed as an argument would be read as several commands.
.TP
\fBdispatch\fR <func>[,arg...] [client,<id>]
Invoke an internal compositor function.
The function name and its arguments are separated by commas.
Optionally add \fBclient,<id>\fR (before or after the function) to target a
specific client.
.br
Examples:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBamsg dispatch toggle_floating\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBamsg dispatch move_window,10,100\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBamsg dispatch move_window,10,100 client,4\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBamsg dispatch toggle_idle_inhibit\fR \- flip the manual idle inhibit
(keep the screen awake); append \fB,1\fR or \fB,0\fR to force a state
instead of toggling
.RE
.TP
\fBget config\-schema\fR [\fIgroup\fR]
Describe every settable option: its type, range, enum members, default, a label
and a one\-line explanation. Compile\-time constant, so a client should cache it
and revalidate with \fBget config\-schema\-digest\fR. An optional \fIgroup\fR
restricts the reply to one settings page.
.TP
\fBget config\-schema\-digest\fR
Return just the schema's digest, so a cached copy can be revalidated in one
small request across a compositor restart.
.TP
\fBget config\fR [\fIgroup\fR]
Return the current value of every described option, each with \fBsource\fR:
which file and line set it, the path it was actually written at, and whether
that file may be written back to. Colours carry both the \fB0xRRGGBBAA\fR form
and four floats. \fBkind\fR is \fBfile\fR, \fBdefault\fR, or \fBruntime\fR --
the last meaning \fBset_option\fR changed it in memory and a reload will undo
it.
.TP
\fBget dispatch\-actions\fR
Return every dispatchable action with the \fIkinds\fR of its arguments, so a
keybind editor can offer the right control instead of a text box.
.SS "Window rules and keybinds"
.TP
\fBget window\-rule\-schema\fR
Describe every window\-rule field: type, range, group, label and explanation,
plus both spellings (\fBnice\fR is the canonical dashed KDL name, \fBkey\fR the
bare one the legacy form uses). Two flags matter and neither is derivable from
the parsed rule: \fBregex\fR marks the matchers, and \fBtristate\fR marks the
fields that are unset/0/1 rather than boolean.
.TP
\fBget window\-rules\fR
Return the current rules, each reporting \fIonly the fields it actually sets\fR --
which is what distinguishes "says nothing about blur" from "turns blur off".
Values come back in the spelling they were written in, so \fBtags 4\fR reports
\fB4\fR and not the bitmask the field holds.
.TP
\fBget binds\fR
Return every keyboard and mouse bind with the chord verbatim, the dispatch
\fIname\fR, the raw argument strings, the keymode, and the four
\fBlock\fR/\fBkeysym\fR/\fBrelease\fR/\fBpass\fR flags. Served from records
captured while the config is read, because the parsed \fBKeyBinding\fR is a
function pointer and a union by then. \fBsource.editable\fR is false for binds
written in the legacy line form -- they are listed but cannot be rewritten --
and \fBnot_listed\fR names the kinds with no KDL handler at all.
.TP
\fBcapture\-chord\fR
Wait for a key combination and print it, e.g.
\fB{"ok":true,"chord":"SUPER+SHIFT+Q"}\fR. The reply is \fIdeferred\fR: nothing
is sent until a key is pressed, so this blocks.
.RS
.PP
Capturing happens in the compositor because bindings are taken before the focused
surface sees them \(em a client reading its own key events would receive
everything \fIexcept\fR the combinations already bound, which is exactly the set
you reach for when rebinding.
.PP
A bare modifier keeps the capture open. The captured chord is swallowed, so a
bound combination does not also run while it is being captured, and works again
immediately afterwards. Unmodified \fBEscape\fR cancels; a second client gets
\fBbusy\fR.
.RE
.SS "Writing rules and binds"
.TP
\fBset\-window\-rules\fR <\fIjson\fR>
.TP
\fBset\-binds\fR <\fIjson\fR>
Add, change or remove window rules and keybinds. The body is a JSON object of
operations:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{"changes":[{"op":"update","index":3,"fields":{"appid":"mpv","force_hdr":"1"}},
            {"op":"add","fields":{"appid":"kitty","isterm":"1"}},
            {"op":"remove","index":5}]}

{"changes":[{"op":"add","chord":"Super+X","action":"spawn","args":["kitty"]},
            {"op":"update","index":2,"chord":"Super+Q",
             "action":"kill_client","flags":{"release":true}}]}
.fi
.PP
\fBindex\fR is the position reported by \fBget window\-rules\fR or
\fBget binds\fR. An \fBadd\fR needs no index; an \fBupdate\fR replaces the
declaration wholesale, so it must carry every field that should survive.
.PP
Nested KDL is written and the existing reader flattens it, so there is one
parsing path rather than a writer that also has to know the legacy comma form.
Edits replace the node's bytes: comments survive, a removal takes the comment
lines directly above it, and every touched file is re\-parsed before any of them
is renamed into place.
.PP
The batch is all\-or\-nothing. A bind naming a dispatch the compositor does not
know is refused with \fBunknown\-action\fR rather than written into a config
that fails at the next login. A rule or bind written in the legacy line form has
no node to replace and is refused with \fBnot\-editable\fR.
.PP
There is no \fBpersist\fR: a window rule takes effect when a window maps and a
keybind is a lookup, so there is nothing to preview. The config is re\-read
afterwards \fIwithout\fR respawning the \fBspawn\fR list, which a full
\fBreload_config\fR would do.
.RE
.SS "Writing config (set\-config)"
.TP
\fBset\-config\fR <\fIjson\fR>
Apply a batch of config changes and write them to the file that declares them.
The body is a JSON object:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{"changes":[{"path":"effects/blur/radius","value":"8"},
            {"key":"cursor_theme","value":null}],
 "persist":true,"override":false}
.fi
.PP
Either \fBkey\fR (the internal name) or \fBpath\fR (the nested KDL path)
identifies an option. \fBvalue\fR of \fBnull\fR removes the declaration and
reverts to the compiled\-in default.
.PP
The batch is all\-or\-nothing: every change is resolved and validated before
anything is touched, and a change that did not run reports
\fBnot\-applied\fR rather than success. Out\-of\-range values are refused,
not clamped, and the reply carries the bounds.
.PP
\fBpersist\fR defaults to true. \fBfalse\fR applies in memory only -- the
live\-preview path -- and the value then reads as \fBruntime\fR in
\fBget config\fR.
.PP
A key whose value comes from a generated file (a matugen palette, say) is
refused with \fBread\-only\-source\fR. \fBoverride\fR:\fBtrue\fR appends a
shadowing block to the main config instead, never to the generated file.
.PP
Edits are surgical: comments survive, every file is re\-parsed before it is
renamed into place, and the previous contents are kept at \fI<file>.bak\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBset\-config @\-\fR
Read the JSON body from standard input instead of from the command line, which
avoids quoting it through a shell and lifts the 4KB argument limit:
.RS
.PP
.nf
printf '{"changes":[{"key":"borderpx","value":"3"}]}' | amsg set\-config @\-
.fi
.RE
.SS "Persistent streams (watch)"
\fBWatch\fR commands keep the connection open and continuously output JSON
updates. The initial state is sent immediately, followed by updates whenever
relevant changes occur.
.TP
\fBwatch monitor\fR <name>
Stream updates for the named monitor.
.TP
\fBwatch focused\-client\fR
Stream updates for the focused client.
.TP
\fBwatch client\fR <id>
Stream updates for the client with the given ID.
.TP
\fBwatch tags\fR <monitor>
Stream tag updates for the named monitor.
.TP
\fBwatch all\-monitors\fR
Stream updates for all monitors.
.TP
\fBwatch all\-tags\fR
Stream updates for all tags.
.TP
\fBwatch all\-clients\fR
Stream updates for all clients.
.TP
\fBwatch idle\fR
Stream idle-inhibit changes, in the shape \fBget idle\fR returns. Pushed when
any field changes -- including when the manual flag flips while a client
inhibitor already holds idling off, which changes nothing about sleeping but is
still what a pill showing that flag has to redraw from, and including a second
portal request arriving while one already holds it, which moves only the list.
.TP
\fBwatch keymode\fR
Stream keymode changes.
.TP
\fBwatch keyboardlayout\fR
Stream keyboard layout changes.
.TP
\fBwatch last_open_surface\fR [\fImonitor\fR]
Stream last open surface changes for a specific monitor (or the focused one).
.TP
\fBwatch bar\-config\fR
Stream the resolved theme, re-sent on every config reload -- so a bar in
another process repaints with a new palette rather than waiting for something
else to wake it. This carried the whole of a `bar {}` block until that block was
removed; how the bar looks is the bar's own config now.
.TP
\fBwatch config\fR
Stream config changes as a DIFF: only the keys that changed, with the same
\fBsource\fR detail \fBget config\fR carries. The first push after subscribing
is everything (\fBreason\fR is \fBinitial\fR); after that \fBreason\fR is
\fBreload\fR or \fBset\fR. A change that changes nothing pushes nothing --
matugen fires on every wallpaper change and often lands on the same colours.
.SS "Output configuration"
.TP
\fBset_output_mode\fR,<output>,<WxH[@Hz]>
Set a mode the output reports. Tested before it is committed; a mode that is
not advertised is refused.
.TP
\fBset_output_scale\fR,<output>,<scale>
Set the scale, fractional included.
.TP
\fBset_output_transform\fR,<output>,<0-7>
Rotate or flip the output, live: 0 normal, 1 90, 2 180, 3 270, 4 flipped,
5 flipped-90, 6 flipped-180, 7 flipped-270. The attachment keeps the mode's
extents at every transform; only the presentation extents swap.
.TP
\fBset_output_mode_transform\fR,<output>,<WxH[@Hz]>,<0-7>
Set the resolution and the transform in ONE commit, so that the frame drawn
for them has both pending at once. A custom mode is accepted for outputs that
advertise no mode list.
.TP
\fBset_output_position\fR,<output>,<x>,<y>
Move the output within the layout.
.TP
\fBset_output_vrr\fR,<output>,<0|1>
Enable or disable adaptive sync.
.TP
\fBset_output_icc\fR,<output>,<path>
Load an ICC profile for SDR output; an empty path clears it.
.SS "Dispatch actions"
The full list, as the compositor reports it \- this section is GENERATED from
\fBasteroidz \-D\fR, which is what it must be checked against: a hand\-kept
copy of 94 names drifts the first time one is added.
.PP
Each is invoked as \fBamsg dispatch <name>[,<arg>...]\fR. The angle brackets
give the KIND of each argument, not a literal: \fIdirection\fR is one of
left/right/up/down, \fItag\-index\fR is 1\-9, \fIoption\-key\fR is any key
from \fBamsg get config\-schema\fR. \fBamsg get dispatch\-actions\fR returns
the same table as JSON and is what a program should read.
.PP
Arguments are comma\-separated with no spaces, and a \fBclient,<id>\fR prefix
targets a window other than the focused one \- see \fBdispatch\fR above.
.PP
One of them does not drive the session but looks at it.
\fBdump_blur_source\fR,\fIprefix\fR[,\fIframes\fR] writes the next few frames'
backdrop\-blur SOURCE images \- the scene replay a window's shadow is about to
blur, before it blurs it. One \fIprefix\fR\-<n>\-live<k>.pam per blur node, where
<k> is its command index, plus \fIprefix\fR\-<n>\-cache\-plain.pam and
\fIprefix\fR\-<n>\-cache\-dark.pam on any frame that rebuilt the monitor
background blur cache; each has a .txt sidecar giving the crop and the blur's
write box in output coordinates. With no argument it disarms; it also stops on
its own after \fIframes\fR (1\-60, default 3).
.PP
Note that \fIframes\fR cannot be given over IPC: the comma is this protocol's own
argument separator, so only \fIprefix\fR arrives and the count stays at 3. Use a
keybind argument, or \fIAZ_BLUR_DUMP_FRAMES\fR with \fIAZ_BLUR_DUMP\fR to arm at
startup instead.
.PP
It exists because that image never reaches the screen and the blur averages away
whatever was wrong with it, so the artefact and its cause never appear in the
same screenshot. Each armed frame waits for its own submission before reading it
back, so it stutters by design, and it writes wherever it is pointed with the
compositor's privileges. See \fBdocs/visuals/effects.md\fR.
